00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:35.640
[Narrator:] The great out-of-doors. Restful,
calm, majestic, there's nothing like it to

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:40.800
give you the feeling that it's great to be alive.
Everyone shares in the feeling.

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:48.300
Everyone that is except Ed Parmalee. Ed has a problem.
No, it isn't Marion, his wife, it's this.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:58.329
Ed doesn't know what's making that noise.
What's more, he's afraid to find out. Why?

00:00:58.329 --> 00:01:04.180
He's scared it might be something serious
and his fear has distorted his judgment.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:08.670
He knows something is the matter with the
engine, but because he's scared of what it

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:15.600
might be, he won't admit anything is wrong.
That's what a simple fear can do.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:18.060
 Here are some other things it can do.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:20.130
[Marion:] Ed, what's that noise?

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:22.499
[Ed:] Noise? What noise?

00:01:22.499 --> 00:01:27.490
[Narrator:] Ed's not really an idiot; he
is simply denying the source of anxiety, pretending

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:32.700
there's nothing there. Fear can also make
us project the anxiety. Try to laugh it all.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:36.160
[Marion:] It's the kind of knock, knock,
knock, knock.

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:43.160
[Ed:] Oh, I get it, knock, knock, who's there?
Marion. Marion who? Marion Hayes, repent at leisure.

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:44.820
[Laughter]

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:48.800
[Narrator:] Or if that doesn't work there's
the camouflage called icy disdain.

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:52.300
[Marion:] Why don't you go back to Glassner's Garage?

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:55.900
[Music]

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:01.000
Now, Ed, you're just being stubborn. Just
the way you are about your stomach trouble.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:03.800
[Ed:] Oh, for crying out loud, lay off will you?

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:07.500
[Narrator:] And that was the last refuge
in ducking the issue -- anger.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:13.800
Unreasoning anger, but you can't drive away anxiety
about your car or your stomach

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:18.200
by denial, sarcasm or anger. There are always reminders.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.400
[Music]

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:24.400
[Billboard promoting cancer screening
looms ahead of Bill and Marion.]

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:28.200
[Camera zooms in on symptom number 5: Persistent Indigestion.]

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:33.000
[Ed:] Forget it.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:39.100
[Narrator:] And there comes a time when it's
impossible to ignore the trouble any longer.

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:44.280
[Car's engine begins sputtering. ]

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:51.200
[Ed stops the car, gets out, and opens the hood.]

00:02:57.500 --> 00:02:59.640
[Narrator:] So you may want advice. It's easy to get.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:04.010
[Man standing behind Ed as he peers under car hood:]
Looks bad. Once they start to go it's curtains.

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:07.000
[Second man:] Have luck, try the crank shaft.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:09.280
[Third man:] Nay, rebore the widget fittings.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:13.000
[Fourth man:] My brother-in-law had the same trouble.
Blooey!

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:19.900
[Narrator:] Ed is now more confused and worried
than he was before. But what's this?

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:26.200
Hmm, save expensive repair bills, recondition your
own motor. Results guaranteed.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:33.100
Well, there are the results and they're guaranteed.

00:03:33.109 --> 00:03:35.840
[Marion:] Now will you take it to Glassner's, Ed?

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:40.500
[Mechanic:] Well, I tell you what I'd do,
Mac, a place down the road a ways,

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:47.000
a fellow name of Clyde has a special system for taking
a motor down. Save you some dough.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:49.640
[Narrator:] This is Clyde.

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:53.900
[A figure with an evil smile, clad in green,
slides out from under a car and backs Ed's car into the garage.]

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:58.030
[Narrator:] Got a special system for taking a motor apart.

00:03:58.030 --> 00:04:04.000
[Clyde holds a hand grenade which he tosses into the hood of the car. An explosion is heard from inside the garage.]

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:09.800
[Clyde:] Little worse shape than I figured Jack.
I've got to put a king-sized ring flangers [inaudible] pump

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:12.400
suppressors and miller casing mount.

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:14.549
[Ed:] Uh-huh.

00:04:14.549 --> 00:04:18.400
[Narrator:] Ed didn't save a dime by going to Clyde.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:23.000
[Music]

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:26.809
[Man:] Too bad, Mr. Parmalee. Wish you'd
brought it in when the trouble first started.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:29.680
Now..

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:36.600
Well, here's the tariff. We replaced
it. What's the matter, Mr. Parmalee?

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:38.880
Something bothering you?

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:42.300
[Narrator:] Yes, something is bothering Ed.
His stomach.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:46.000
 As Marion said, it's been on the blink for a couple of weeks now.

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:49.800
Again, Ed's worried and scared.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:56.000
Worried that his indigestion might be something serious,
and scared to face the problem.

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:57.700
 So, when the subject comes up.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:00.659
[Marion:] Why do you take that stuff all the time, Ed?

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:04.200
[Narrator:] He uses the same bag of tricks: denial.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:07.400
[Ed:] What do you mean? I don't take it all the time.

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:09.000
[Narrator:] Laughing it off.

00:05:09.409 --> 00:05:11.000
[Marion:] I don't think it does you any good.

00:05:11.009 --> 00:05:14.879
[Ed:] Well, well, the Florence Nightingale of Blake Street. 
[Laughter]

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:17.500
[Marion:] We were talking about your stomach.

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:19.800
[Narrator:] Icy disdain.

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:26.500
[Music]

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:27.610
[Narrator:] And finally anger.

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:31.060
[Marion:] Ed, I wish you'd go and see a doctor.

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:32.400
[Ed:] Get out!

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:34.979
[Narrator:] Just a minute, Ed .

00:05:34.979 --> 00:05:35.959
[Ed:] Huh?

00:05:35.959 --> 00:05:38.159
[Narrator:] You're being pretty difficult you know.

00:05:38.159 --> 00:05:39.710
[Ed:] I'm being difficult?

00:05:39.710 --> 00:05:43.590
[Narrator:] Right. The same as you were about your uhh car.

00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:45.600
[Ed:] Oh.

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:48.500
[Narrator:] You know your body is a lot like your car motor

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:54.000
except that it's a little harder to buy new parts for it.
When your motor is in good shape, it runs like this.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:55.800
[humming sound]

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:58.600
When something goes wrong, it usually gives you a warning.

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:00.500
[clanking sound] 

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:04.300
Your body usually gives you a warning too. 

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:07.200
When it does, it's best to pay attention and do something about it.

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:12.080
[Ed:] Do something? My medicine cabinet is loaded with stuff.

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:17.490
[Narrator:] Ed, using that in your stomach
is like using this in your engine. It's dangerous.

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:21.600
Look, Ed, you've had indigestion for more than a couple of weeks.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:26.080
Remember that sign board? It's something that could mean cancer.

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:32.600
[Ed:] Yeah. Well, I might as well give up then. 
Like the fellows were saying just the other day.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:36.949
[Man 1:] Cancer, once it starts in, it's curtains.

00:06:36.949 --> 00:06:38.050
[Man 2:] Runs in the family.

00:06:38.050 --> 00:06:39.419
[Man 3:] Contagious too.

00:06:39.419 --> 00:06:42.619
[Man 4:] My wife's uncle had it.
Blooey!

00:06:42.619 --> 00:06:46.800
[Narrator:] You've heard amateur diagnoses before.
You ought to know what it's worth.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:48.000
Nothing.

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:49.800
[Ed:] Well what can I do?

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:56.300
[Narrator:] Very simple. Get your clothes on
and go right now to see a doctor.

00:06:56.700 --> 00:06:59.800
[Ed:] Yeah, yeah.

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:04.400
[Narrator:] It's perfectly right and normal
to be afraid of cancer.

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:08.500
Cancer is an enemy, but you don't fight it by getting panicky.

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:11.300
Instead you take positive action.

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:13.900
You go to a man who has the weapons to fight the enemy.

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:17.000
Wait a minute.

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:21.500
Ed, Ed, that's not the man.

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:24.500
Remember Clyde the mechanic with the special system? 

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:27.300
This is the same type of operator.

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:32.000
Yes, the only man to trust is a recognized physician. 

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:35.100
Ed's still pretty nervous.

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:37.600
[Nurse:] All right, Mr. Parmalee.

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:41.400
[Narrator:] You're always more scared of
something you know nothing about

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:44.200
and Ed has a lot to learn about cancer.

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:45.900
Here are the facts.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:47.800
Cancer is not contagious.

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:50.100
Cancer is not hereditary.

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:52.300
Cancer is curable.

00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:58.800
Thousands of Americans are cured of cancer every year
by one or more of three recognized cures;

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:03.800
surgery, x-ray and radium.

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:13.600
You see, a cancer is a group of cells within your body
whose growth is uncontrolled and very rapid.

00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:19.619
At first the cancer may be localized,
but unless its growth is checked quickly

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:21.400
it spreads.

00:08:21.800 --> 00:08:27.100
It may spread this way by direct extension,
or which is even more dangerous,

00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:31.500
 it may spread like this 
with cells leaving the main part of the cancer

00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:35.600
and entering the bloodstream
to lodge and grow in other parts of the body.

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:38.200
This is called metastasis.

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:41.000
It can happen to a cancer at any time.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:45.600
When it does, the problems of locating and attacking
the cancer are greatly increased.

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:51.400
Cancer must be found and treated before
it extends too far or metastasizes.

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:57.400
Early examination is half the battle
in successful cancer treatment.

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:00.000
That means going to a doctor.

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:04.379
With new scientific methods,
we can accurately detect cancer much

00:09:04.379 --> 00:09:07.400
earlier than we could even five years ago.

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:10.200
But in order to get to a doctor early, 

00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:14.800
we must constantly be on the lookout for cancer's danger signals.

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:16.700
Watch for these.

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:23.000
Any sore that does not heal.
A lump or thickening in the breast or elsewhere.

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:25.800
Unusual bleeding or discharge.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:28.900
Any change in a wart or mole.

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:32.700
Persistent indigestion or difficulty in swallowing.

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:35.000
Persistent hoarseness or cough.

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:38.000
Any change in normal bowel habits.

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:44.829
Thousands of people are enjoying life today
because they went to their doctor's early,

00:09:44.829 --> 00:09:49.500
but the fact is that twice as many people
could be cured of cancer every year

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:51.500
 if we would all do these things.

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:53.800
Watch for the danger signals.

00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:56.200
Ignore rumormongers.

00:09:56.200 --> 00:09:58.100
Keep away from quacks.

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:01.800
Put no trust in so-called home remedies for cancer.

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:04.800
Go to the doctor regularly for checkups,

00:10:04.900 --> 00:10:08.800
and go immediately when a symptom shows up
that might mean cancer.

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:14.000
But here's Ed again a few days later 
waiting for the results of his examination.

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:16.259
[Nurse:] All right, Mr. Parmalee.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:20.970
[Doctor:] Well, Ed, here are the answers
you've been waiting for.

00:10:20.970 --> 00:10:26.800
[Narrator:] Ed realizes now how valuable
was the time he wasted in denial, sarcasm, and anger.

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:30.800
[Doctor:] Fear and worry. To get to
the point you're most concerned about though, Ed, 

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:33.100
you do not have cancer.

00:10:33.500 --> 00:10:35.500
[Ed:] Ed: I don't! I don't! 

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:40.500
Oh, what a knucklehead I've been.
Oh, why didn't I get down here weeks ago?

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:43.300
Oh, boy! No more worrying.

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:47.600
[Narrator:] Hold it, Ed. It is foolish to
worry day and night about cancer,

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:50.600
 but it's just as foolish not to worry about it at all.

00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:55.400
Be on guard. Don't let fear make a mess of your life again,

00:10:55.400 --> 00:10:58.000
but use your good common sense.

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:02.000
Well, Ed's not making the same mistake twice.

00:11:02.050 --> 00:11:07.600
Now, he watches for warning signals and goes
to a doctor every six months for a checkup

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:11.939
no matter how well he may feel.
Oh, yes, and so does Marion.

00:11:11.939 --> 00:11:17.259
What a difference it makes ensuring
their health, their peace of mind, their dispositions.

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:20.900
Ed and Marion are able to enjoy life to its fullest.

00:11:20.900 --> 00:11:26.700
[Music]

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:29.500
[The End. UPA. United Productions of America.]

00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:35.600
[This film was made possible by your contributions
to the American Cancer Society]

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:36.600
[from the film library of the American Medical
Association]

